   "version": "v0.4.68",
   "title": "Byte Quest: model family built (staged), byte dataset, causality test passes; predictions registered",
   "date": "2026-11-22",
   "html": "<p>### Byte Quest build (E12–E16; subagent, verified by the main agent)</p><p><strong>What</strong>:</p><ul><li>The byte-level FusionDance family, as <strong>staged patches</strong> (<code>notes/patches/byte-model.patch</code>, <code>byte-train.patch</code>; <code>git apply --check</code> OK; applied between campaigns) plus a CPU-testable working copy in <code>experiments/byte_model/</code>.</li><li>Variants: flat byte-E1; patched coarse-to-fine with P = 2/4/8/16, the one-patch causal shift, a byte encoder, an E1-style global body on patches, expansion, and a shared byte refinement loop × r (gated or contractive updates, re-injection, pass embedding, per-pass loss); an AR or <strong>parallel_refine</strong> decoder; optionally a looped or ternary global core.</li><li>30 staged configs in <code>experiments/staged-configs/byte/</code> (200M-byte and 20M-byte variants). Every config sets <code>max_seq_len</code> to its byte context (the RoPE cap from the 20:07 review). The windows match across the granularity sweep (1,024 bytes); byte layers are GDN + conv.</li></ul><p><strong>Dataset <code>code-py-v1-bytes</code></strong>: the same docs and split as code-py-v1 (val 4,000 docs / 3.94M bytes; train 987,154 docs / 976.9M bytes; uint8; EOS = 0x00, never inside a doc). <strong>Every doc round-trips byte for byte</strong>, and the clean-val ids map one-to-one (the manifest sha256 matches). The locked test splits weren't read.</p><p><strong>SQ206 causality: PASS.</strong> 42 variant × P combinations and 412 perturbations at patch starts, middles and ends are all bitwise-unchanged. The <strong>broken no-shift control leaks in every case</strong> (4–11 of 8–11 perturbations).</p><p><strong>Tests</strong>: <code>test_byte_model.py</code> <strong>24/24 OK</strong> (re-run by the main agent, 44 s). Defaults are bit-identical to <code>gogeta.model</code> for E1/E2/E3/E11-style configs. The trainer smoke test passes on CPU.</p><p><strong>Costs</strong> (estimates, training FLOPs per BPE-token-equivalent):</p><ul><li>E12 flat 325M (3.57× E1);</li><li>E13 P = 2/4/8/16: 172/95/60/43M;</li><li>E14 r = 2 shared = 2-pass unshared 109M, r = 4 138M;</li><li>E15 parallel r = 1/2/3: 95/112/129M;</li><li>E16 looped 204M, ternary 109M.</li></ul><p>They match <code>notes/analysis-coarse-to-fine.md</code>. About 7 GPU-h per seed overall (to be replaced by SQ189's measurements).</p><p><strong>Predictions REGISTERED</strong> (<code>experiments/checks/PREDICTIONS.md</code>, \"Registered Byte Quest predictions E12–E16\"; plan <code>notes/byte-quest.md</code>), e.g.:</p><ul><li>E12 1.15–1.40× BPE-E1 bits/char at matched FLOPs;</li><li>E13: P = 4 best or tied with P = 2, P = 16 ≥ 5% worse;</li><li><strong>E14: shared r = 2 within ±0.5% of equal-FLOP unshared;</strong></li><li>E15: parallel r = 2 within 1% of AR;</li><li>E16: ternary global core +3–9%.</li></ul><p>Every byte result is reported per doc-length bucket (C193).</p><p><strong>Not verified</strong>: anything on GPU (fla, bf16, compile, throughput, memory); byte LRs (SQ188 runs first); E12 needs the flex-attention patch.</p><p>### Also</p><ul><li><code>notes/compute-rental.md</code>: a Vast.ai price snapshot (19:50). The RTX 3090 (~$0.20/hr median) is the cheapest card that runs everything; 8× is recommended; renting is the human's call.</li><li><strong>SQ220</strong> cloud pilot (parked for the human).</li><li><strong>SQ219</strong> context-removal intervention (from the 19:44 ML-theorist review).</li></ul>"
