   "html": "<p><strong>What</strong>: registers <strong>E10</strong>: Hugging Face's reference <code>LlamaForCausalLM</code> (transformers 5.24.0) at E0's exact size, trained by our own loop, data, eval and tracking. <strong>Not run yet.</strong></p><ul><li><code>gogeta/train.py</code>: a <code>\"arch\": \"hf_llama\"</code> config key (default <code>fusiondance</code>), an <code>HFLlama</code> adapter with loss computed identically, FLOPs by the same rule as FusionDance, and a <code>--lr</code> override. Run configs record <code>arch</code> and <code>cli_overrides</code>.</li><li><code>configs/e10_llama_ref.json</code>, <code>experiments/launch/E10.sh</code>, card, plain-English page, registry entry with its <strong>prediction</strong>; E9's card, and a note that E9 runs at E1's tuned LR.</li></ul><p>### E10: Reference control: Hugging Face Llama at E0's size</p><ul><li><strong>Question:</strong> what does a well-known reference implementation reach on our data with a tuned LR? Is our E0 baseline weak?</li><li><strong>Result:</strong> not run. It runs in the Phase 2 campaign: K7 LR sweep, then 3 seeds.</li><li><strong>Verdict vs prediction:</strong> prediction (registry): 3-seed mean 0.595–0.615, seed sd ≤ 0.004; E0 at the same LR within mean ± max(3 sd, 0.5%); E1 still beats it by 1.5–4%.</li><li><strong>What it means:</strong> E10 becomes <strong>the</strong> control for every comparison once it lands.</li><li><strong>Card:</strong> experiments/cards/E10.md</li></ul>"
